WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.125
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.125 --> 00:00:03.780
MIKE SHORT: Instead
of saying, analyze

00:00:03.780 --> 00:00:06.570
this theoretical problem, I
said, analyze your toenails.

00:00:06.570 --> 00:00:09.690
Tell me how much arsenic and
gold you've got in your body.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:12.660
All we study at MIT is the
natural world and things

00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:15.930
we make out of it, so everything
is reducible to practice.

00:00:15.930 --> 00:00:19.440
Everything can be real,
if you put in the effort.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.060
SARAH HANSEN: Today
on the podcast,

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:23.760
we're talking about
ionizing radiation

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.660
and nuclear engineering.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:29.000
How do you make these
things real and tangible?

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.360
MIKE SHORT: Matter
is a form of energy.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:33.180
And once that
clicked, everything

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:34.830
seemed to make
sense-- radioactive

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.350
decay, nuclear reactions,
all these things.

00:00:37.350 --> 00:00:40.800
I remember that aha
moment in this class

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.220
that I teach as a second-year
student back in 2002.

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:45.840
And it's those kind
of moments that

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:48.132
made me want to stay in it,
because I feel like, wow, I

00:00:48.132 --> 00:00:50.215
really know this field now.

00:00:50.215 --> 00:00:52.050
SARAH HANSEN: Welcome
to Chalk Radio,

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:54.750
a podcast about inspired
teaching at MIT.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:59.650
I'm your host, Sarah Hansen
from MIT OpenCourseWare.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:02.930
The Nuclear Engineering and
Ionizing Radiation course

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:07.010
at MIT take students from
understanding basic physics

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:10.040
to grappling with the core
concepts of Einstein's E equals

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.660
mc squared.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.490
In this episode,
we're going to delve

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:15.530
into how this is
possible, and what

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:19.160
it takes to make a class that's
not only hands-on, but also

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.590
capable of evolving daily.

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:24.410
My guest is one of the main
people that makes this happen,

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:26.410
Professor Mike Short.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:29.090
MIKE SHORT: This course
is all about radiation,

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:32.310
both its origins and its uses.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:36.080
So this is the first course on
its intro to everything nuclear

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.200
that any student
at MIT would take.

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:41.060
And a lot of times for students,
it's their first modern physics

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:41.930
course.

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.420
The physics courses that
first-year students take

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:48.380
are often things that we've
known for 100 to 300 years.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:51.740
And the field of nuclear
physics is still evolving.

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:55.160
We're still using
nuclear radiation spectra

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.490
to detect the presence of
water on Mars or the moon.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:00.530
We're still confirming
our knowledge

00:02:00.530 --> 00:02:02.780
of which particles do
and don't exist and why.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.666
So this is also intro
to modern physics.

00:02:06.666 --> 00:02:11.240
SARAH HANSEN: So nuclear science
and radiation in particular

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.210
are emotionally charged
topics, you know?

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:18.410
You read on the internet claims
like, cell phones cause cancer,

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:19.820
things like that.

00:02:19.820 --> 00:02:23.420
How are you preparing students
to debunk pseudoscience

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.545
and to really serve the public?

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:27.170
MIKE SHORT: We actually
spent two weeks

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:29.570
at the end of the class
looking at studies

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:32.690
that are false or have
exaggerated claims

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.742
and teaching students
what to look for.

00:02:34.742 --> 00:02:36.200
So the first 11
weeks of the class,

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:38.240
we teach the students
the fundamentals

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:39.590
of nuclear science.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:42.500
And then we turn to published
articles, and blogs,

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.210
and other things in the field.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:46.340
And we debunk myths
like cell phones

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.760
cause cancer due to
ionizing radiation.

00:02:48.760 --> 00:02:51.050
Cell phones don't emit
ionizing radiation.

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:54.020
We debunk myths like,
the tiniest little bit

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:57.230
of irradiation can harm
you, when in truth, we

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:59.690
don't have the data for that.

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:04.640
But a lot of misinformation in
radiation and nuclear science

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.558
is incorporated into
culture, into our sort

00:03:08.558 --> 00:03:10.100
of collective
consciousness, and even

00:03:10.100 --> 00:03:12.950
in what's called the linear no
threshold model, which says,

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:15.360
every little bit of
radiation does harm.

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:17.090
We don't know that
to be true or false,

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.382
and it's a good thing we don't.

00:03:18.382 --> 00:03:21.110
Because we would need to
have exposed tens of millions

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:22.685
of people to low
levels of radiation

00:03:22.685 --> 00:03:24.560
in a controlled study,
which is not something

00:03:24.560 --> 00:03:26.760
I think is ethically
correct to do.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:29.000
It's also not ethically
correct to say

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:32.490
that all radiation causes
harm, because we don't know.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:35.960
And I want students to both
recognize false science

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:37.790
in the field, and
recognize when we

00:03:37.790 --> 00:03:40.820
don't know enough information
to say something confidently,

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:43.260
and be comfortable with
that lack of knowledge.

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:45.407
It means there's
something new to explore.

00:03:45.407 --> 00:03:47.240
But if you don't have
something to conclude,

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:48.240
don't draw a conclusion.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:49.970
SARAH HANSEN: Uh-huh.

00:03:49.970 --> 00:03:52.730
How does this connect
to the irradiated fruit

00:03:52.730 --> 00:03:55.330
party that have in the class?

00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:55.872
What is that?

00:03:55.872 --> 00:03:56.580
MIKE SHORT: Yeah.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:59.390
The last day of class, we often
have an irradiated fruit party,

00:03:59.390 --> 00:04:01.070
where I bring in
fruit that could only

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:04.740
be brought into the US
because it's irradiated.

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.897
So there are many
fruits that are--

00:04:06.897 --> 00:04:08.480
there many different
types of produce,

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.490
including fruits,
that are irradiated,

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:14.660
and it's the only known way to
kill all of the insect, viral,

00:04:14.660 --> 00:04:18.860
and bacterial pathogens that
can wreak havoc on either people

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:20.690
or on our crops.

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:24.260
An interesting point
of information, Hawaii

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.930
and Puerto Rico, despite
being part of the US,

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:29.510
are agriculturally
distinct areas,

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:30.950
and you are not
allowed to simply

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:32.210
import produce from those.

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:35.030
I had an apple confiscated
from the airport in Puerto Rico

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:37.410
when I learned that
to be the case.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.110
However, if you
irradiate foods, like,

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:42.050
this is why we can get a lot
of pineapples from Costa Rica.

00:04:42.050 --> 00:04:43.580
We've started
getting mangosteens

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:46.160
in from Thailand, where I
didn't know what that fruit was

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:47.930
until a few years
ago and now, you

00:04:47.930 --> 00:04:50.450
can find them at H
Mart in Cambridge.

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:53.390
A lot of this is because
we can kill the pests,

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:54.920
and it doesn't harm the food.

00:04:54.920 --> 00:04:57.170
It doesn't make the
food radioactive.

00:04:57.170 --> 00:05:00.120
But a lot of this is to
personalize the science.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:02.510
So when students eat
food that they may or may

00:05:02.510 --> 00:05:06.450
not known have been
irradiated, they taste good.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:07.280
They seem safe.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:09.890
And it's one of those things
where once it's personalized,

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:11.390
it's not as scary.

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:14.540
When you learn the knowledge and
then you see it for yourselves,

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:16.272
it becomes a lot
more acceptable.

00:05:16.272 --> 00:05:18.230
SARAH HANSEN: Yeah,
learning through experience

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.690
is very powerful.

00:05:20.690 --> 00:05:22.550
What does it mean
to you for students

00:05:22.550 --> 00:05:24.910
to develop fluency
in this field?

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:27.200
MIKE SHORT: It's important
to be fluent in this field

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.780
because a cursory knowledge
of radiation science

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:31.200
is not enough.

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.535
I'd say there are a lot of
self-proclaimed experts--

00:05:34.535 --> 00:05:36.470
I call them armchair PhDs--

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:39.980
who have learned a bit of
genuine knowledge, but then

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.990
extrapolate it too far.

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:43.880
And that combined
with all the things

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.550
we've heard in pop
culture, unfortunately

00:05:46.550 --> 00:05:49.670
sometimes from celebrities
spouting falsehoods

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:52.220
about radiation, or
vaccines, or other things

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:55.700
that they don't understand,
people listen to other people,

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.800
and people listen to role models
and folks that they look up to.

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:00.920
But it's important to be
fluent and well-grounded

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:04.620
in the fundamentals so that you
can sort out fact from fiction.

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:06.830
And I want every student
that leaves my class

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:09.300
to be able to recognize
something that's incorrect,

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.800
even if it's told to them by a
celebrity, an expert, a parent,

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.260
a friend, anyone--

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.380
that they know what the reality
is, and it shouldn't depend

00:06:18.380 --> 00:06:19.613
on the source it comes from.

00:06:19.613 --> 00:06:21.780
They should be able to tell
whether it's real or not

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.120
and verify if the
source is genuine.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:24.140
SARAH HANSEN: Uh-huh.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:27.110
And how do you help students
develop this fluency?

00:06:27.110 --> 00:06:29.690
MIKE SHORT: So it starts
off with the fundamentals

00:06:29.690 --> 00:06:30.740
of radiation science.

00:06:30.740 --> 00:06:33.410
So like any class, we
teach all the fundamentals

00:06:33.410 --> 00:06:35.450
from well-established theory.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:39.760
But along the way every week, we
have labs and personalisation.

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:41.510
Like, for example, the
first day of class,

00:06:41.510 --> 00:06:43.635
I ask students to bring in
their toenail clippings.

00:06:43.635 --> 00:06:44.510
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.190
And they usually say,
that's disgusting.

00:06:46.190 --> 00:06:46.880
What are we doing?

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:47.630
And I say, you'll see.

00:06:47.630 --> 00:06:49.340
We're going to put
them in the reactor.

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:51.140
And we irradiate their
toenail clippings.

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:53.930
And because to some degree,
you are what you eat,

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:57.590
some of the elements which
we eat and we don't want to,

00:06:57.590 --> 00:06:59.570
things like arsenic,
or selenium,

00:06:59.570 --> 00:07:02.780
or chromium, some of which
can be good in small amounts,

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.015
bad in large amounts--

00:07:04.015 --> 00:07:05.390
others like arsenic,
I'm not sure

00:07:05.390 --> 00:07:06.920
if there's a good use of it--

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:09.230
get incorporated
into our toenails.

00:07:09.230 --> 00:07:12.500
So we activate those toenails
by putting them in the reactor.

00:07:12.500 --> 00:07:16.220
They absorb neutrons and give
off characteristic gamma rays,

00:07:16.220 --> 00:07:18.710
giving away how many
atoms of arsenic,

00:07:18.710 --> 00:07:21.200
and selenium, and such are
incorporated into the toenails

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:23.570
with striking precision.

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:26.840
And so we're able to tell
where students come from based

00:07:26.840 --> 00:07:28.760
on analysis of their toenails.

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:33.650
We had one student who had a
lot of gold in their toenails.

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.160
And I said, I thought I asked
you guys to clean these off,

00:07:37.160 --> 00:07:37.930
remove any polish.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.110
And the student
said, yeah, I did.

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:43.340
But I live near a gold mining
town, and it's in the water.

00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.048
SARAH HANSEN: Wow,
that's so interesting.

00:07:45.048 --> 00:07:46.965
MIKE SHORT: So that's
what I mean by personal,

00:07:46.965 --> 00:07:48.950
is they discover
things about themselves

00:07:48.950 --> 00:07:50.270
through nuclear science.

00:07:50.270 --> 00:07:52.430
In the problem sense,
instead of saying,

00:07:52.430 --> 00:07:54.380
analyze this theoretical
problem, I said,

00:07:54.380 --> 00:07:55.430
analyze your toenails.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:07:58.175
Tell me how much arsenic and
gold you've got in your body.

00:07:58.175 --> 00:07:59.720
SARAH HANSEN: Right, right.

00:07:59.720 --> 00:08:02.133
So in the course,
you make a point

00:08:02.133 --> 00:08:03.800
of saying that the
method of instruction

00:08:03.800 --> 00:08:09.500
is often context first, theory
second, and then context again.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:12.200
How does that relate to
that method of instruction?

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.060
MIKE SHORT: This is an example
of that method of instruction.

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:17.120
I like to start by
opening knowledge gaps

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:19.670
rather than spouting
theory that someone.

00:08:19.670 --> 00:08:22.540
It doesn't usually stick if I
just say, here are some facts.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:23.040
Learn them.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.748
That's usually in one
ear, out the other,

00:08:24.748 --> 00:08:26.022
if they're listening at all.

00:08:26.022 --> 00:08:27.980
But when you show someone
something surprising,

00:08:27.980 --> 00:08:29.210
they're fully engaged.

00:08:29.210 --> 00:08:30.910
They're always
multi-sensory engaged.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:32.515
They're listening.

00:08:32.515 --> 00:08:34.640
In a lot of cases, they're
touching, in some cases,

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:35.468
even smelling.

00:08:35.468 --> 00:08:37.010
Taste is the sense
that we don't tend

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:39.620
to engage in nuclear
science, with good reason.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:40.710
[CHUCKLES]

00:08:40.710 --> 00:08:43.610
But you can sense, and
feel, and hear a lot

00:08:43.610 --> 00:08:44.960
of things in nuclear science.

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:49.400
Like yesterday, I was with
one of my graduate students.

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:52.160
We were looking at some
highly irradiated materials

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:55.010
for a reactor in Idaho, and
we heard this little faint

00:08:55.010 --> 00:08:57.630
buzzing noise in
the Geiger counter.

00:08:57.630 --> 00:08:59.630
And if you put your ear
up to the Geiger counter

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:01.400
near the radiation
source, you can hear

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:02.900
tiny electrical discharges.

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:04.940
You can hear the
detector working.

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:08.210
And then I want the student
to say, why is that?

00:09:08.210 --> 00:09:10.820
Why do I hear this fuzzy
noise near the detector

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:11.990
when it's working?

00:09:11.990 --> 00:09:15.380
Then when you explain why,
students tend to remember.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:17.870
Not too many people learn
well by being lectured at,

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:20.660
but everyone learns well
by opening knowledge gaps.

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:24.890
And you're effectively pulling
the information in rather

00:09:24.890 --> 00:09:26.815
than us pushing it
to the students.

00:09:26.815 --> 00:09:28.190
Something I learned
from a mentor

00:09:28.190 --> 00:09:30.400
here is you can't push a string.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:32.150
You want knowledge
into a student's brain,

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:33.108
they've got to pull it.

00:09:33.108 --> 00:09:35.126
You can't push it.

00:09:35.126 --> 00:09:38.560
SARAH HANSEN: You made a choice
in this iteration of the course

00:09:38.560 --> 00:09:43.070
to offer students the ability
to do analytical homework

00:09:43.070 --> 00:09:45.650
or take-home, hands-on labs.

00:09:45.650 --> 00:09:46.598
How did that work out?

00:09:46.598 --> 00:09:48.140
What does that look
like in practice?

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:50.390
MIKE SHORT: Interestingly,
I spent all this time

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:52.460
making these optional labs.

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.330
Nobody did them.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:09:56.270
So the next couple
of years, I simply

00:09:56.270 --> 00:09:58.130
made everything mandatory.

00:09:58.130 --> 00:10:00.067
The students said they
loved the flexibility.

00:10:00.067 --> 00:10:02.150
They're really psyched
that I put in all this time

00:10:02.150 --> 00:10:03.500
to do the labs.

00:10:03.500 --> 00:10:05.550
But it wasn't for a grade,
so they didn't do it.

00:10:05.550 --> 00:10:06.350
And so that's when I learned.

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:08.450
If it's not graded, it's
not going to get done.

00:10:08.450 --> 00:10:10.250
So I made all the
labs mandatory.

00:10:10.250 --> 00:10:12.320
I cut out a little
bit of the analytics

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:15.740
in favor of adding context
before and after the theory,

00:10:15.740 --> 00:10:17.720
and retention went up.

00:10:17.720 --> 00:10:19.550
Grades went up, on average.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.680
And the course
evaluations went up, too.

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:24.520
So anything numerical
we can get improved--

00:10:24.520 --> 00:10:26.740
and in my subjective
opinion, so did

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:29.750
the students' knowledge
of what's happening.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:31.270
And that, I get
from my colleagues,

00:10:31.270 --> 00:10:34.690
because I track these students
as they progress through MIT,

00:10:34.690 --> 00:10:35.950
through our department.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:38.590
And my colleagues who
teach further-on courses,

00:10:38.590 --> 00:10:40.810
the more advanced ones,
can tell me whether or not

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:42.518
the students really
know the fundamentals

00:10:42.518 --> 00:10:43.750
that they're depending on.

00:10:43.750 --> 00:10:45.417
So far, things have
been getting better,

00:10:45.417 --> 00:10:47.135
but it requires planning.

00:10:47.135 --> 00:10:48.760
And it also requires
a lot of thinking,

00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:50.620
where I'll look
through my syllabus,

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:53.613
and I have an empty column
where the user doesn't

00:10:53.613 --> 00:10:55.780
exist in most syllabi, which
is, what is this week's

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:56.980
hands-on instruction?

00:10:56.980 --> 00:10:58.780
And I try to make
sure that's full.

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:01.580
So another example is
if you want to know,

00:11:01.580 --> 00:11:03.580
do you have real diamond rings?

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:05.860
When we get to reading
electron spectra

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:07.702
and characteristic
X-ray spectra,

00:11:07.702 --> 00:11:09.910
I could either give them a
problem from theory, which

00:11:09.910 --> 00:11:12.970
is boring, or I can run
some standard for them,

00:11:12.970 --> 00:11:14.650
where they know what to expect.

00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:16.630
Or I can say, that's
a nice diamond ring.

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:18.680
Do you want to
know if it's real?

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:20.797
And the student invariably
says, absolutely, I

00:11:20.797 --> 00:11:21.880
want to know if it's real.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:22.660
SARAH HANSEN: Right.

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:24.077
MIKE SHORT: So we
have the student

00:11:24.077 --> 00:11:26.320
take the controls of
the electron microscope

00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:30.770
and analyze it to see, does that
diamond emit zirconium x-rays?

00:11:30.770 --> 00:11:33.160
Because if it does,
it's cubic zirconia.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:36.580
If it emits silicon x-rays, it's
moissanite or silicon carbide.

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:39.340
My favorite one was
this day happened

00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:40.750
to fall on parents' weekend.

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:43.180
So I asked the students, does
anybody have a diamond ring?

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:45.180
And one of the students'
mothers said, oh, yeah.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:46.930
Let's check my engagement ring.

00:11:46.930 --> 00:11:49.302
And her husband
was just, oh, gosh.

00:11:49.302 --> 00:11:50.260
What's going to happen?

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:51.218
What's going to happen?

00:11:51.218 --> 00:11:52.765
He thinks he bought
a genuine ring.

00:11:52.765 --> 00:11:53.902
It turned out to be real.

00:11:53.902 --> 00:11:54.610
We had the proof.

00:11:54.610 --> 00:11:55.860
SARAH HANSEN: OK, that's good.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:57.385
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:11:58.255 --> 00:12:02.380
Mike, can you tell us about
the radioactive scavenger hunt?

00:12:02.380 --> 00:12:03.700
MIKE SHORT: Sure.

00:12:03.700 --> 00:12:06.370
I challenge the students to
find the most radioactive place

00:12:06.370 --> 00:12:07.590
in Boston.

00:12:07.590 --> 00:12:09.688
And each of them had
to go in teams of two

00:12:09.688 --> 00:12:11.980
and pick a place that they
thought would be radioactive

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:15.370
based on what we'd learned
about where you find radiation.

00:12:15.370 --> 00:12:18.610
So radiation, a lot of
it comes from space,

00:12:18.610 --> 00:12:21.100
from cosmic protons
that hit the atmosphere.

00:12:21.100 --> 00:12:23.810
So some students thought, I'll
go to the tallest building,

00:12:23.810 --> 00:12:25.810
and I'll probably
get more radiation.

00:12:25.810 --> 00:12:28.060
Others had read about
radon underground,

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:31.905
because there are isotopes
of radium emitting radon gas.

00:12:31.905 --> 00:12:34.030
And so they thought, we'll
go down into the subway,

00:12:34.030 --> 00:12:35.620
get as low as we can go.

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:38.320
Other students looked at the
relative amount of radiation

00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:41.800
in different building materials,
like wood, clay, marble,

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:45.970
granite, and they went to the
most granite-dense locations

00:12:45.970 --> 00:12:48.610
they could find, or the
ones with the most marble.

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:50.488
And those are the
students that won.

00:12:50.488 --> 00:12:52.030
There were places
in Boston that have

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:55.252
six times the normal
radiation background,

00:12:55.252 --> 00:12:57.460
simply because they're made
out of marble or granite.

00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:59.168
These include things
like the state house

00:12:59.168 --> 00:13:01.150
and some fancy
fountains around town.

00:13:01.150 --> 00:13:02.660
Did not know about
the fountains,

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:05.650
but they just thought, let's
find giant chunks of stone,

00:13:05.650 --> 00:13:06.670
and they were right.

00:13:11.440 --> 00:13:13.420
SARAH HANSEN: The
hands-on experiences

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:15.880
that Mike creates for
students of his course

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:17.770
are pretty unique.

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:19.810
He told me that when
he took this course

00:13:19.810 --> 00:13:22.300
as an undergraduate
student at MIT,

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:24.730
it wasn't typically hands-on.

00:13:24.730 --> 00:13:26.830
So I wanted to know
what it's like to teach

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:29.410
in a way that's so different
from his own personal

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:30.650
experience.

00:13:30.650 --> 00:13:34.300
What does it take to create such
fascinating labs and lessons

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:37.750
without a clear model from one's
own educational background?

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:39.280
MIKE SHORT: It's natural.

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:42.280
I teach the way that I
learn, because I thought back

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:45.100
on all my experiences and I
thought, from which courses

00:13:45.100 --> 00:13:47.860
did I really remember a lot?

00:13:47.860 --> 00:13:50.290
And these were things like
hands-on blacksmithing

00:13:50.290 --> 00:13:52.540
or laboratory courses.

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:55.210
We did have a lab class where
we counted a lot of radiation,

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:57.470
and I remember those
labs very well.

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:00.920
And I think back to my
neutronics problem sets.

00:14:00.920 --> 00:14:02.890
I remember the theory
OK, but I don't

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:06.280
remember very many visual
instances of that class.

00:14:06.280 --> 00:14:07.610
It just kind of happened.

00:14:07.610 --> 00:14:09.360
The knowledge is maybe
in there somewhere.

00:14:09.360 --> 00:14:09.970
I don't know.

00:14:09.970 --> 00:14:11.650
But I know where
I was when I did

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:13.670
most of the hands-on exercises.

00:14:13.670 --> 00:14:16.570
And in the end, you can
make anything hands-on, even

00:14:16.570 --> 00:14:18.620
neutronics that I mentioned.

00:14:18.620 --> 00:14:21.310
So I at some point
went skulking around

00:14:21.310 --> 00:14:23.710
places I oughtn't, like
around in the reactor

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:28.060
once I got access, and found
an eight-foot pile of graphite

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:29.840
that was behind a
bunch of equipment.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:30.610
It wasn't hidden.

00:14:30.610 --> 00:14:32.598
It was just covered with junk.

00:14:32.598 --> 00:14:33.640
And I asked, what's that?

00:14:33.640 --> 00:14:35.950
They just said, oh, that's our
subcritical graphite reactor

00:14:35.950 --> 00:14:36.460
pile.

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:38.290
We're going to get
rid of that next year.

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:41.260
So I sounded the alarm and
said, you cannot get rid of this

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:42.460
graphite pile.

00:14:42.460 --> 00:14:44.710
And then our neutronics
professors Ben Forget and Kord

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:45.580
Smith said, yeah, you're right.

00:14:45.580 --> 00:14:46.330
We can't.

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:48.787
So they spent a whole
winter restoring it

00:14:48.787 --> 00:14:49.870
with a couple of students.

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:52.000
And now, it's one of the
central labs in my class

00:14:52.000 --> 00:14:53.330
and in their class.

00:14:53.330 --> 00:14:55.690
So we've taken the most
theory-heavy, dry, and boring

00:14:55.690 --> 00:14:58.390
class and turned it
experimental because you can.

00:14:58.390 --> 00:15:01.000
You're always studying
the natural world, right?

00:15:01.000 --> 00:15:03.940
All we study at MIT is the
natural world and things

00:15:03.940 --> 00:15:07.210
we make out of it, so everything
is reducible to practice.

00:15:07.210 --> 00:15:11.686
Everything can be real,
if you put in the effort.

00:15:11.686 --> 00:15:14.470
SARAH HANSEN: Part of what's
so special about this class is

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:16.450
the dedication that
Mike and his colleagues

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:19.300
have to constantly improving
it through real-time student

00:15:19.300 --> 00:15:20.360
feedback.

00:15:20.360 --> 00:15:22.480
And I don't mean fixing
pieces to implement

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:23.950
for the next semester.

00:15:23.950 --> 00:15:26.080
I'm talking about
next-day transformations

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:27.760
of class procedures.

00:15:27.760 --> 00:15:32.555
To accomplish this, Mike created
the aptly titled Rants Page.

00:15:32.555 --> 00:15:35.800
MIKE SHORT: The Rant Page is an
anonymous, simple, PHP comment

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:39.220
form that I wrote, where I
want students to tell me things

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:41.140
that they want changed.

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:44.950
Because I try my best to
collect in-person feedback

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:47.993
from the students both
one-on-one and in class.

00:15:47.993 --> 00:15:49.660
But some students
don't feel comfortable

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:52.400
telling a professor, I don't
like what you're doing.

00:15:52.400 --> 00:15:55.660
So I give them a place to do
so completely anonymously.

00:15:55.660 --> 00:15:57.520
It ended up being
20 lines of code.

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:58.850
It wasn't hard.

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:02.440
And what I started getting
was real-time feedback about,

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:03.880
I can't read your writing.

00:16:03.880 --> 00:16:06.130
So then I know to slow it down.

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:08.860
Or, I really wish you
wouldn't slow the class down

00:16:08.860 --> 00:16:11.380
for this one student's
incessant questions,

00:16:11.380 --> 00:16:13.690
so I know to limit each
student to a few questions

00:16:13.690 --> 00:16:15.580
if it gets to be too much.

00:16:15.580 --> 00:16:17.110
And I would address
them in class

00:16:17.110 --> 00:16:19.630
to say, it's safe to address
this, because it's anonymous.

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:21.710
I have literally
no way of knowing.

00:16:21.710 --> 00:16:24.010
But if one person wrote
it, probably a lot of you

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:25.300
are thinking it.

00:16:25.300 --> 00:16:27.190
And the students responded
positively to say,

00:16:27.190 --> 00:16:28.898
wow, it was really
nice to know that we'd

00:16:28.898 --> 00:16:30.947
make a suggestion at
2:00 in the morning,

00:16:30.947 --> 00:16:33.280
and then by 10:00 in the
morning, it would be addressed.

00:16:33.280 --> 00:16:34.570
The class would
change in real time,

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:37.240
and they knew they had the power
to shape their own learning.

00:16:37.240 --> 00:16:40.060
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:16:43.350 --> 00:16:45.490
SARAH HANSEN: With all the
buzz around this course,

00:16:45.490 --> 00:16:48.180
we had a ton of great questions
come in from educators

00:16:48.180 --> 00:16:49.720
and students alike.

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:51.390
So we picked out
some of our favorites

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:53.770
and posed them to Mike.

00:16:53.770 --> 00:16:59.542
Number one, what math do I
need to understand this field?

00:16:59.542 --> 00:17:01.000
MIKE SHORT: That's
a good question.

00:17:01.000 --> 00:17:04.000
It depends on how deeply you
want to understand the field.

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:05.500
If you want to pass
my class, if you

00:17:05.500 --> 00:17:07.839
want a get an A in my
class, you don't really

00:17:07.839 --> 00:17:10.599
need much math beyond
single-variable calculus.

00:17:10.599 --> 00:17:13.250
And even then,
it's not very much.

00:17:13.250 --> 00:17:14.680
I think we use--

00:17:14.680 --> 00:17:17.470
we have one or two lectures with
integrals and a few lectures

00:17:17.470 --> 00:17:20.470
with differential equations,
but linear first order things

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:22.927
that you solve in calculus one.

00:17:22.927 --> 00:17:26.140
SARAH HANSEN: Number two,
when you were a student,

00:17:26.140 --> 00:17:28.750
how did you deal
with courses that

00:17:28.750 --> 00:17:33.032
didn't seem interesting to
you, but that you had to study?

00:17:33.032 --> 00:17:34.490
MIKE SHORT: That's
a good question.

00:17:34.490 --> 00:17:35.990
I have a few answers to that.

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:38.800
So for courses that
didn't seem interesting

00:17:38.800 --> 00:17:41.740
that I had to study, if I
knew why I had to study it,

00:17:41.740 --> 00:17:46.430
there was at least a
practical reason to do well.

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:48.760
For example, for me,
it was neutronics.

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:50.380
Neutron transport
is one of the things

00:17:50.380 --> 00:17:52.870
that makes nuclear
engineers what they are.

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:57.820
I found it to be dry
and not very real-world,

00:17:57.820 --> 00:18:00.730
because I knew I was never
going to be a reactor designer.

00:18:00.730 --> 00:18:03.940
But I felt I would need to
understand neutron transport

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:06.640
and power levels in order to be
an effective nuclear material

00:18:06.640 --> 00:18:07.510
scientist.

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:08.873
Luckily, I was right.

00:18:08.873 --> 00:18:11.290
For the classes which I just
had to take because they were

00:18:11.290 --> 00:18:15.220
requirements, and I had no
reason to want to take them,

00:18:15.220 --> 00:18:16.750
I got a little sneaky.

00:18:16.750 --> 00:18:20.110
I ended up double majoring
with material science

00:18:20.110 --> 00:18:23.350
and wrote a petition to get
out of this one medical imaging

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:25.600
class and replace
it with 12 others

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:27.318
in order to make a second major.

00:18:27.318 --> 00:18:28.610
And that petition was approved.

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:32.320
So I actually did get to simply
drop a departmental requirement

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:35.822
by articulating why I wanted
to study something else.

00:18:35.822 --> 00:18:37.780
Not all students realize
that they can do this,

00:18:37.780 --> 00:18:39.370
but they can do this.

00:18:39.370 --> 00:18:42.130
With a very good
intellectual justification,

00:18:42.130 --> 00:18:44.540
rules can be bent or broken.

00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:47.150
SARAH HANSEN: OK, number three.

00:18:47.150 --> 00:18:50.770
Why can't we just send
nuclear waste to space?

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:53.290
MIKE SHORT: We could just
send nuclear waste to space

00:18:53.290 --> 00:18:55.180
and get it out of our hair.

00:18:55.180 --> 00:18:56.660
It would be expensive.

00:18:56.660 --> 00:18:59.440
It costs a lot of money
per gram to get something

00:18:59.440 --> 00:19:00.850
off the planet.

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:03.130
Someone has to
agree to pay for it.

00:19:03.130 --> 00:19:06.040
And what worries me most is,
what if one of those missions

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:07.310
goes wrong?

00:19:07.310 --> 00:19:09.790
What if you're launching a
rocket full of the world's

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:12.190
worst nuclear
waste, and something

00:19:12.190 --> 00:19:15.700
goes wrong at the launch, and
then it comes back down, along

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:16.900
with the rocket explosion?

00:19:16.900 --> 00:19:19.780
Then you have
contaminated the planet.

00:19:19.780 --> 00:19:23.230
So I personally believe in
containing nuclear waste where

00:19:23.230 --> 00:19:26.170
we can see it rather
than blasting it off

00:19:26.170 --> 00:19:28.720
into space and
contaminating space,

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:32.643
unless we know where it's going,
and that nothing will go wrong.

00:19:32.643 --> 00:19:34.060
Because a lot of
folks are worried

00:19:34.060 --> 00:19:35.810
about the dangers
of radiation, how

00:19:35.810 --> 00:19:37.750
we're going to deal
with nuclear waste.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:40.540
And I don't fear
nuclear waste, but I've

00:19:40.540 --> 00:19:42.550
got a healthy respect
for it in that whatever

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:46.060
we do with it has to have the
lowest probability of getting

00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:47.980
out and contaminating anything.

00:19:47.980 --> 00:19:50.350
I think it's a necessary
thing to make in order

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:52.250
to make nuclear power.

00:19:52.250 --> 00:19:56.292
So if we want to make almost
unlimited carbon-free power,

00:19:56.292 --> 00:19:58.000
we're going to make
waste in the process.

00:19:58.000 --> 00:19:59.292
You can't fight thermodynamics.

00:19:59.292 --> 00:20:01.833
You're always going to have some
waste of energy or something

00:20:01.833 --> 00:20:02.450
else.

00:20:02.450 --> 00:20:03.460
But then what you
do with it has to be

00:20:03.460 --> 00:20:04.940
very carefully considered.

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:07.100
And it sounds simple
to blast it into space,

00:20:07.100 --> 00:20:09.100
but then you have to
think, what could go wrong,

00:20:09.100 --> 00:20:11.590
and who could I hurt
if it goes wrong?

00:20:11.590 --> 00:20:13.780
SARAH HANSEN:
Number four, what do

00:20:13.780 --> 00:20:16.240
you think about the
cultural and political idea

00:20:16.240 --> 00:20:17.980
against nuclear power?

00:20:17.980 --> 00:20:21.370
MIKE SHORT: To me, the current
cultural and political idea

00:20:21.370 --> 00:20:24.220
against nuclear power
is not grounded in fact.

00:20:24.220 --> 00:20:25.762
It's grounded in emotion.

00:20:25.762 --> 00:20:27.220
And I've talked
with a lot of folks

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:30.010
who either know very
little or very much

00:20:30.010 --> 00:20:33.520
about the physics and
engineering of nuclear power.

00:20:33.520 --> 00:20:35.080
But I find more
often than not, it's

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:38.050
an issue designed
to rally a base.

00:20:38.050 --> 00:20:41.230
Strangely, I've never
really understood this.

00:20:41.230 --> 00:20:45.445
So many environmentalists
are against nuclear power.

00:20:45.445 --> 00:20:46.820
And I'm an
environmentalist, too,

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:48.820
which is why I'm
for nuclear power.

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:51.070
So I find the
anti-nuclear sentiment

00:20:51.070 --> 00:20:55.570
to be so strongly democratic
and the pro-nuclear sentiment

00:20:55.570 --> 00:20:58.930
to be so strongly
Republican, neither of which

00:20:58.930 --> 00:21:02.380
is for reasons which
I'm willing to accept.

00:21:02.380 --> 00:21:08.230
They seem to be more about
political tribalism than fact.

00:21:08.230 --> 00:21:11.230
And it's interesting now that
for the first time since we've

00:21:11.230 --> 00:21:14.023
had Chernobyl
disasters and such,

00:21:14.023 --> 00:21:15.940
more and more environmentalists
are coming out

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:18.880
in favor of nuclear
power, not because they're

00:21:18.880 --> 00:21:21.560
in favor of radiation,
and waste, and such.

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:24.970
But the goal is to
prevent climate change.

00:21:24.970 --> 00:21:28.330
I would much sooner
take a risk of something

00:21:28.330 --> 00:21:31.240
going wrong with nuclear
power than definitely lose

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:33.160
the battle to climate change.

00:21:33.160 --> 00:21:37.630
Everything to me comes out to
minimizing risk to human life

00:21:37.630 --> 00:21:40.820
and maximizing quality of life.

00:21:40.820 --> 00:21:43.420
So to me, the risk
of nuclear power

00:21:43.420 --> 00:21:46.370
is that if we can go all
carbon-free for energy,

00:21:46.370 --> 00:21:48.670
we can reverse climate change.

00:21:48.670 --> 00:21:51.520
If we're afraid of using
nuclear power for fear

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:55.150
of the waste getting out,
or the risk, or the weapons,

00:21:55.150 --> 00:21:58.550
then we're automatically
losing the war,

00:21:58.550 --> 00:22:01.120
and we're going to have an
uninhabitable planet anyway.

00:22:01.120 --> 00:22:02.662
And we can't get
off this planet yet,

00:22:02.662 --> 00:22:04.578
and then we'll go make
the same mistakes there

00:22:04.578 --> 00:22:05.530
as we would here.

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:07.720
For example, I came into
this department wanting

00:22:07.720 --> 00:22:10.870
to study fusion, felt it
wasn't ready yet, so I spent

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:12.700
a lot of my time on
fission, thinking,

00:22:12.700 --> 00:22:14.740
this is going to be
the bridge to fusion.

00:22:14.740 --> 00:22:17.860
Because fusion promises
more carbon-free power

00:22:17.860 --> 00:22:20.700
with far less radioactive
waste, but not none.

00:22:20.700 --> 00:22:22.605
And I'm willing to accept
the some, so as not

00:22:22.605 --> 00:22:24.730
to lose the climate change
battle, which is already

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:26.350
on our doorstep.

00:22:26.350 --> 00:22:28.240
I do worry that many
environmentalists

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:32.650
lose sight of the real goal,
which is protecting the planet.

00:22:32.650 --> 00:22:36.250
And to me, protecting the
planet doesn't mean, do no harm.

00:22:36.250 --> 00:22:38.800
It means, do as little harm
as possible while preserving

00:22:38.800 --> 00:22:41.368
our quality of life.

00:22:41.368 --> 00:22:45.670
SARAH HANSEN: Number five, while
learning, occasionally, you

00:22:45.670 --> 00:22:48.360
will have these moments where
all of the sudden, the dots

00:22:48.360 --> 00:22:51.390
suddenly connect, and a
previously challenging topic

00:22:51.390 --> 00:22:54.120
becomes seemingly
perfectly clear to you.

00:22:54.120 --> 00:22:57.906
Could you share with us
one of your brain blasts?

00:22:57.906 --> 00:22:58.870
MIKE SHORT: Let's see.

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:01.630
For me, it's the same one
that most students get

00:23:01.630 --> 00:23:04.660
at about the one-month
mark in my class,

00:23:04.660 --> 00:23:10.090
and that's energy is matter,
that E equals mc squared.

00:23:10.090 --> 00:23:12.130
You see it on
shirts all over MIT.

00:23:12.130 --> 00:23:16.150
It's probably the one equation
that everyone in America knows,

00:23:16.150 --> 00:23:18.910
but not a lot of people
really understand

00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:22.240
that the conversion
of matter into energy

00:23:22.240 --> 00:23:26.080
through ionizing radiation
is the movement of energy

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:27.130
from one form to another.

00:23:27.130 --> 00:23:29.060
Matter is a form of energy.

00:23:29.060 --> 00:23:30.850
And once that
clicked, everything

00:23:30.850 --> 00:23:32.500
seemed to make
sense-- radioactive

00:23:32.500 --> 00:23:35.020
decay, nuclear reactions,
all these things.

00:23:35.020 --> 00:23:38.500
I remember that aha
moment in this class

00:23:38.500 --> 00:23:42.610
that I teach as a second-year
student back in 2002.

00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:44.230
And it's those kind
of moments that

00:23:44.230 --> 00:23:46.350
made me want to stay in it,
because I feel like, wow, I

00:23:46.350 --> 00:23:47.475
really know this field now.

00:23:47.475 --> 00:23:50.266
